@page "/docs/architecture/data-access"

@* Data access by render mode, the overview: the interface the pages ask, what each render mode needs behind it,
   and which of the two patterns an app needs. The patterns are in DataAccessServer.razor and
   DataAccessBrowser.razor. Patterns only: the framework's own details are linked on Microsoft Learn. The
   store's shape follows izzy add crud's (catalog/crud/files/Data). *@

<ArticlePage Section="architecture" Slug="data-access">
    <Lead>
        A page that runs on the server can read your database. A page that runs in the browser can't, so it calls an
        API. If your pages load data through an interface, the same page works in both places.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">One interface for every mode</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Put the queries behind an interface, a store, and have the page ask it. The page doesn't know where the
            store gets its rows:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="ICustomerStore.cs">@Interface</Code>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Customers.razor">@Page</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The render mode decides which class answers. There are four render modes and two places a page runs, so
            there are two classes to write at most.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">What each render mode needs</Heading>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Render mode</th><th>The page runs</th><th>What answers the interface</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Static</td><td>On the server</td><td>A store over your <code>DbContext</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Interactive Server</td><td>On the server, for as long as its circuit</td><td>The same store, with a context for each call</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>WebAssembly</td><td>In the browser, after prerendering on the server</td><td>A client store that calls your endpoints, and the server’s store while prerendering</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Auto</td><td>On the server first, then in the browser</td><td>Both stores, each registered in its own project</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/architecture/data-access-server">Data access on the server</Anchor>: the store for Static and Interactive Server pages.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/architecture/data-access-browser">Data access in the browser</Anchor>: the client store, its endpoints and their checks, for WebAssembly and Auto pages.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Which pattern an app needs</Heading>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>The app's pages</th><th>What to build</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Static, Interactive Server, or both</td><td>A store over your <code>DbContext</code>. No API.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Mostly static, with a few pages in the browser</td><td>The same store, plus endpoints and a client store for the data those few pages use.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>WebAssembly or Auto throughout</td><td>Endpoints and a client store for all of the app's data.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            Use the interface in a server-only app too. To move a page to the browser later, you add a client store and
            its endpoints, and the page doesn't change.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>: what each render mode is, and how to choose one for a page.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/data-api">Data from an API</Anchor>: a grid's sort, filters and paging over HTTP, and what the server limits.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Interface = """
        public interface ICustomerStore
        {
            Task<IReadOnlyList<CustomerRow>> ListAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);
            Task<CustomerRow?> GetAsync(int id, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);
        }

        public sealed record CustomerRow(int Id, string Name, string City);
        """;

    private const string Page = """
        @page "/customers"
        @inject ICustomerStore Customers

        <Table Items="_customers">
            <Columns>
                <PropertyColumn Property="c => c.Name" />
                <PropertyColumn Property="c => c.City" />
            </Columns>
        </Table>

        @code {
            private IReadOnlyList<CustomerRow> _customers = [];

            protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync() => _customers = await Customers.ListAsync();
        }
        """;
}
